data.content:
Here are the three breaking changes affecting your code in **EF Core 10**, with fixes:

---

## 1. `ExecuteUpdateAsync` — no longer accepts expression trees

The parameter changed from `Expression<Func<...>>` to `Func<...>`. Your manual expression tree won't compile.

**Fix** — use a regular lambda with imperative logic:

```csharp
await context.Blogs.ExecuteUpdateAsync(s =>
{
    s.SetProperty(b => b.Views, 8);
    if (nameChanged)
    {
        s.SetProperty(b => b.Name, "foo");
    }
});
```

---

## 2. Complex type columns are now auto-uniquified

`ShippingAddress.Street` and `BillingAddress.Street` previously shared the column `Street`; now they get `Street` and `Street1`. Nested types also change: `Order.Payment.BillingAddress.City` was `BillingAddress_City`, now becomes `Payment_BillingAddress_City`.

**Fix** — explicitly set column names to preserve old schema:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Customer>(b =>
{
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.ShippingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
    b.ComplexProperty(c => c.BillingAddress, p =>
        p.Property(a => a.Street).HasColumnName("Street"));
});

// For nested complex types:
modelBuilder.Entity<Order>()
    .ComplexProperty(o => o.Payment)
    .ComplexProperty(p => p.BillingAddress)
    .Property(a => a.City)
    .HasColumnName("BillingAddress_City");
```

Or generate a new migration if the new naming is acceptable.

---

## 3. `IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger` — new `logCommandText` parameter

All methods (`CommandReaderExecuting`, `CommandReaderExecuted`, etc.) now require an additional `string logCommandText` parameter (contains potentially-redacted SQL for logging).

**Fix:**

```csharp
public class MyLogger : IRelationalCommandDiagnosticsLogger
{
    public InterceptionResult<DbDataReader> CommandReaderExecuting(
        IRelationalConnection connection,
        DbCommand command,
        DbContext context,
        Guid commandId,
        Guid connectionId,
        DateTimeOffset startTime,
        string logCommandText)   // ← new parameter
    {
        // Use logCommandText for logging (redacted),
        // command.CommandText for actual SQL
    }
}
```

---

**Summary:** The expression-tree change (#1) is the most impactful code simplification; the complex-type changes (#2) can silently break your schema if you don't pin column names or add a migration; and the logger change (#3) is a straightforward signature update.
